{
  "dom_id": "step-q79581",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\,\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{x}{y}).$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{3}-(tx)^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{3}{y}^{3}-{t}^{3}{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{3}({y}^{3}-{x}^{3})}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y)={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-1.$",
